# heavy / binary artifacts: they live in the durable archive, not in git
research-tmp/harvest-*/adapters/
research-tmp/session-*.jsonl
research-tmp/*.pdf
resbench/*.pdf
**/__pycache__/
v2/.selftest/
*.zstd
*.safetensors
*.gguf
v2/data/mixture/
v2/data/corpus/

# review-round scratch: the rebuilt mixtures and change dumps each round produced. They are ~200 MB apiece and were
# being committed on every `git add -A`, which is why .git reached 726 MB. The EVIDENCE for those rounds (their
# receipts, logs and diffs) stays tracked; the regenerable bulk does not, matching the convention stated above.
research-tmp/reviews/*_rebuild/
research-tmp/reviews/*_changes.json

# Same convention for the v2 terminal state (2026-09-29): harvest-v2/mixture is 387 MB of training mixtures and
# data/v3 is 894 MB of regenerable bulk; both were untracked-but-addable, so one `git add -A` would have committed
# ~1.3 GB. harvest-v2/adapters is already covered by the `research-tmp/harvest-*/adapters/` rule above and the
# adapter bytes by `*.safetensors`. The EVIDENCE stays tracked: harvest-v2/{evals,receipts,logs}, MANIFEST.sha256.
research-tmp/harvest-v2/mixture/
data/v3/
# The trained adapters themselves: the safetensors are already ignored by `*.safetensors`, which left two 11 MB
# tokenizer.json files (22 MB) as the only addable part. They are regenerable copies of the base tokenizer, not
# evidence — the adapter bytes live on both hosts and in harvest-v2/adapters, and their receipts are tracked.
adapters/
research-tmp/
